---
import Highlight from '../components/Highlight.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { LEGAL, controllerLine, updatedLong } from '../data/legal';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Terms · The Lazy Horseman"
  description="The terms for using The Lazy Horseman's newsletter and its facility booking tool."
>
  <section class="band cream-band page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The rules of the thing</p>
      <h1>Terms of <Highlight>use</Highlight></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        What you can expect from me, what I need from you, and who is
        responsible when something goes wrong.
      </p>
      <p class="legal-date">Last updated {updatedLong}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band cream-band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose legal">
        <h2>Who this agreement is with</h2>
        <p>
          {controllerLine}, at{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${LEGAL.email}`}>{LEGAL.email}</a>. Using the
          newsletter or the booking tool means you accept these terms. If you do
          not, do not use them.
        </p>

        <h2>What the booking tool is</h2>
        <p>
          A shared diary for a yard's arena, school, horsewalker or whatever
          else it lets people book. A yard sets up its facilities, invites its
          riders, and riders take slots. That is the whole of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It is a booking board, not a safety system.</strong> It does
          not know how many horses an arena can safely hold, whether the surface
          is fit to ride on, or whether anybody has actually left. The yard
          remains responsible for the safety of its facilities and for the
          people on them, exactly as it was before.
        </p>

        <h2>Your account</h2>
        <p>
          One account per person. Yards should not share a single login between
          riders, which defeats the point and makes the diary useless. Keep your
          password to yourself, and tell me if you think somebody else has it.
        </p>
        <p>
          You need to be thirteen or over to have an account. Younger riders
          should be booked in by the yard.
        </p>

        <h2>Yards and riders</h2>
        <p>
          A yard decides who is on its list. It can invite people, take them off
          and block them, and it does not need my agreement to do any of it. If
          you are removed from a yard, that is between you and the yard, and I
          will not overturn it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Equally, disagreements about who had the arena, whose fault a clash
          was, or what the yard's own rules are, are for the yard to sort out. I
          keep the diary. I do not referee it.
        </p>

        <h2>Behaving reasonably</h2>
        <p>
          Do not use this to harass anybody, to book slots you have no intention
          of using in order to keep others out, to break into accounts that are
          not yours, or to hammer the service in a way that spoils it for
          everyone else. Any of those and the account goes.
        </p>

        <h2>Plans and paying</h2>
        <p>
          One facility is free, with as many riders as you like, and there is no
          card involved. Paid plans for more facilities are coming, and nothing
          about the free tier changes retrospectively when they arrive.
        </p>
        <p>
          When paid plans start, prices will be shown before you agree to
          anything, billing will be monthly or yearly in advance, and cancelling
          stops the next payment rather than refunding the current period. If
          you drop to a smaller plan, facilities beyond the new allowance are
          not switched off automatically, so nothing disappears from under your
          riders without you deciding it should.
        </p>

        <h2>What I can promise about it working</h2>
        <p>
          I will keep it up and working as well as I reasonably can, but this is
          a small operation and there is no uptime guarantee. It may be
          unavailable for maintenance, and things outside my control will
          occasionally break it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Keep running your yard in a way that survives the diary being down for
          an afternoon.
        </p>

        <h2>Your data</h2>
        <p>
          Your yard's bookings and rider list belong to the yard, not to me. Ask
          and you can have a copy. For anything to do with personal information,
          the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> is the detail, and it is
          part of these terms.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a yard's rider list and its bookings, the yard is the data
          controller and I am the processor. That means I hold that data and
          process it only as the yard directs and as needed to run the service,
          I keep it secure, I use only the companies named in the privacy policy
          to help, and on request I will return or delete it. If your yard needs
          a fuller data processing agreement on paper, ask and you will get one.
        </p>

        <h2>When something goes wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing here limits liability for death or personal injury caused by
          negligence, for fraud, or for anything else the law does not allow to
          be limited.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond that, and because much of this is free: I am not liable for
          lost profit, lost business, or losses arising from a booking that did
          not save, a notification that did not arrive, or the service being
          unavailable. Where liability cannot be excluded, it is capped at what
          you have paid in the twelve months before the problem, or one hundred
          pounds, whichever is greater.
        </p>
        <p>
          The booking tool does not replace insurance, supervision, or a yard's
          own rules about who rides when.
        </p>

        <h2>Ending it</h2>
        <p>
          You can stop at any time. Delete your account and it goes, within
          thirty days, from every yard.
        </p>
        <p>
          A yard closing its account frees its address on this domain after a
          period, and somebody else may then claim it. Export anything you want
          to keep before you close it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I can suspend or close an account that breaks these terms. Except in
          serious cases you will hear why first, and get a chance to put it
          right.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
        <p>
          These will change as the tool grows. The date at the top changes with
          them, and anything that materially affects a yard gets an email before
          it takes effect. Carrying on using the service after that is
          acceptance.
        </p>

        <h2>The legal footing</h2>
        <p>
          These terms are governed by the law of England and Wales, and the
          courts of England and Wales deal with any dispute. If you are a
          consumer, this does not take away rights you have under the law of
          where you live.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing in the newsletter is veterinary advice. If your horse is
          unwell, ring your vet.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
